  "reconstructibility": 1,
  "reason": "The records collectively show that arc melting or water quenching can select metastable or multiphase HEA structures, that Laves phases can coexist with a matrix, and—most importantly in R1—that chemical partitioning and secondary phases in a Fe–Co–Ni-based magnetic HEA can reduce saturation magnetization while increasing coercivity. R7 also provides related finite-temperature sublattice-moment physics. However, none identifies FeCoNiTi fcc/C14 coexistence, proposes a composition-matched C14-dominant control, or connects Ti-sublattice antiferromagnetic coupling, orbital moments, spin canting, and C14-assisted domain-wall pinning to the reported transition temperatures and approximately 667 Oe coercivity. Thus the literature points in the general direction, but the specific intervention and mechanism require a substantial new idea."
